#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092136 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d3383cb-2322-5ed0-bd5b-631596d72af9" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "6fc6a60d-11e9-56c1-9328-0179059d4c56" :question/math-academy-id "e-8389" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\arsinh{2} =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\arsinh x$ is \n\n$$\n\\arsinh{x} =\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\arsinh{2} & = \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{2^2+1}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t   \n& = \\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt{4+1}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t \n&=\\ln\\left(2 + \\sqrt 5\\right).\n\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092137 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69ebb184-52aa-5fde-8956-50b69fde3561" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/id #uuid "a8951542-ff6f-5b96-a287-b4c7f928b572" :question/math-academy-id "e-8390" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\arcosh{3} =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\arcosh{x}$ is \n\n$$\n\\arcosh{x} = \\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\arcosh{3}\n                                                       \n& = \\ln\\left(3+\\sqrt{3^2-1}\\right)\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t   \n& = \\ln\\left(3+\\sqrt{9-1}\\right)\\[3pt]\n\n& = \\ln\\left(3+\\sqrt{8}\\right)\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t \n&=\\ln{\\left(3+2\\sqrt{2}\\right)}.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092138 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d68a11bd-7a2d-5f1f-8be7-c8904fdfb3e7" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"}] :question/id #uuid "13299051-2164-5d54-b90e-52c7a8f1d7f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-8391" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\n$\\artanh\\left ( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right ) =$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nThe definition of $\\artanh{x}$ is \n\n$$\n\\artanh{x} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+x}{1-x}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\artanh\\left ( \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right )\n                \n& = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{1+\\dfrac{1}{2}}{1-\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\n&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln\\left(\\dfrac{\\dfrac{3}{2}}{\\dfrac{1}{2}}\\right)\\[5pt]\n\t\t\n&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\\ln{3}.\n}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092140 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50501d8e-9c4c-5650-a50f-5d847d29435a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Inverse Hyperbolic Function From First-Principles"}] :question/id #uuid "4fbdc166-4225-5fa5-aaa2-0f09073e8e2e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8768" :question/problem "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nUsing the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate $f^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x) = \\sinh{\\left(x^2\\right)}$ and $x \\geq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "$\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}$\n\nLet $y = \\sinh{\\left(x^2\\right)}.$\nSince $x \\geq 0,$ we have $y \\geq 0.$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\sinh{\\left(y^2\\right)},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in[0,\\infty)$ and $y\\in[0,\\infty).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\sinh x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{-{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{{y}^{2}}-{e}^{-{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{{y}^{2}} & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-2x{e}^{{y}^{2}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{{y}^{2}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{{y}^{2}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y = e^{y^2}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{{y}^{2}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{y^2} >0$ for all real $y,$ and $x -\\sqrt{x^2+1} <0,$ we must select the positive branch:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{y}^{2}} & =x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{{y}^{2}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}\\right)}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505792 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d3383cb-2322-5ed0-bd5b-631596d72af9" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06c693b7-cccd-550b-a54d-0a489e678e11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{11}{9}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505119} {:answer/id #uuid "0c559b12-c2b6-5701-81a8-9ef77e5ea06a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505126} {:answer/id #uuid "30e5f82b-22e6-5d14-87b0-25b92fa198e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505189} {:answer/id #uuid "9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505406} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6d3902-4c49-563c-8ffd-9bc430647bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{7}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a953977-58ca-5907-ba22-17c28ab4e885" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505406} :answer-field/id #uuid "5bbba225-3f98-5236-a2d6-45d639d898aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8ce8f55a-871b-590a-9437-e579e7e6b7ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-125838" :question/problem "$$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{9}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\sqrt{\\frac{25}{9}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d3383cb-2322-5ed0-bd5b-631596d72af9" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210bc91a-0886-5112-af4a-e207417a27a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1+\\sqrt{2})t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505159} {:answer/id #uuid "42566f74-ff91-595b-a775-feb8083bfe0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505236} {:answer/id #uuid "5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505298} {:answer/id #uuid "75971b4e-945e-5996-88b2-e5e64c3d4697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505344} {:answer/id #uuid "f4709c83-7348-56c6-8fb5-f7518f7b7264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505581}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ff4e27e-6481-5c9b-833b-fc99bca024b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505298} :answer-field/id #uuid "f35fe716-2eba-5add-9591-f3a00949c2d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fdaedc69-27ce-51b2-8939-51c5e1dbf0b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-125843" :question/problem "$$\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}),$ for $t>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}-2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1}{4{t}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+|\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t>0,$ we have $\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{t}^{2}-1}{2t}+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{2t}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505814 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50501d8e-9c4c-5650-a50f-5d847d29435a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Inverse Hyperbolic Function From First-Principles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2520222e-c4e3-55e1-9c0c-b0256d5a01c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505167} {:answer/id #uuid "6325d0ab-7ae4-5d74-ad04-8971639df056" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505304} {:answer/id #uuid "6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505327} {:answer/id #uuid "cec8de79-540b-5e73-9891-cd653f228d9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505501} {:answer/id #uuid "d5fe4910-17a2-5f7b-b1a8-694b1e285a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-x}{1+x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e4eebfc-75b0-57eb-acfb-cae4a0c06d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505327} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b4efcc4-5cc4-5cf8-81d6-746ced2d80e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505655}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c2e8309b-0e6c-591c-8179-3fd8b7ab6d9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-125981" :question/problem "If $f(x)=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡({x}^{2})$  and $x\\ge 0,$ calculate  ${f}^{-1}(x)$ from first-principles." :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{x}^{2}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on $x\\ge 0.$ We have $y\\in [0,1)$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$ \n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{tanh}⁡{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,1)$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1}{{e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1} \\\\ x({e}^{2{y}^{2}}+1) & ={e}^{2{y}^{2}}-1 \\\\ (x-1){e}^{2{y}^{2}} & +(x+1)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIsolating the ${e}^{2{y}^{2}}$ term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =-\\frac{x+1}{x-1} \\\\ {e}^{2{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{1+x}{1-x} \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}) \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we select the positive root. Therefore,\n\n$$\ny=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x})}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505778 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d68a11bd-7a2d-5f1f-8be7-c8904fdfb3e7" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505110} {:answer/id #uuid "570713fe-220d-50d8-94c9-2619c6bc8a06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2y-1}{2y+1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505280} {:answer/id #uuid "dbc6266d-b0e7-541f-bf6f-0b745cd929ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y-2}{y+2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505527} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc3d996-dd1b-572c-8d28-9f09efecaae3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{y+2}{y-2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505533} {:answer/id #uuid "ed795175-c482-546e-8a40-d08968c9f851" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+y}{2-y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505564}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0302529d-68d6-501c-b539-7e8521229961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505110} :answer-field/id #uuid "021f330a-b40b-5b0d-8fd5-b8401e6cfa50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "670d700d-b88f-53f5-a139-73bdf8c93272" :question/math-academy-id "q-126259" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $y\\in (-2,2),$ which of the following is equivalent to\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2})?$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{y}{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{y}{2})}{1-(-\\frac{y}{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{y}{2}}{1+\\frac{y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{2-y}{2}}{\\frac{2+y}{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2-y}{2+y}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50501d8e-9c4c-5650-a50f-5d847d29435a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Inverse Hyperbolic Function From First-Principles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bf47071-ba79-5799-a1f1-dc8357ef6d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bf47071-ba79-5799-a1f1-dc8357ef6d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505258} :answer-field/id #uuid "65101c58-1cdd-50ae-9a7e-f38ddece8ca6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505652} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dbab2f7-d69d-531a-a607-3574fec6140c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dbab2f7-d69d-531a-a607-3574fec6140c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505218} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fed67e1-a4bc-5b68-9708-4d7e1247bf7b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505662}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2480f399-e4b8-57fb-80b2-6dd5de7e6195" :question/math-academy-id "q-314687" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-3)$ and $x\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x-3).$ Since $x\\ge \\frac{3}{2},$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function, we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2y-3),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge \\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,({e}^{2y-3}+{e}^{-(2y-3)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{2y-3}+{e}^{-(2y-3)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{2y-3} & ={e}^{4y-6}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4y-6}-2x{e}^{2y-3}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{2y-3})^{2}-2x({e}^{2y-3})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{2y-3}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{2y-3} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ 2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since $2y-3\\ge 0,$ the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, so we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})+3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69ebb184-52aa-5fde-8956-50b69fde3561" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39f1a14a-9931-51c6-9320-4b1b53558144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505205} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2d3b25e-e4d9-5fd1-8ed1-6f85c37a6a81" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1131799e-bf6d-5253-8a7c-8f906c272bb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-341314" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "By definition,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=ln\\,(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50}{49}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{50-49}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{49}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{7}+\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}+1}{7}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d68a11bd-7a2d-5f1f-8be7-c8904fdfb3e7" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505254}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a7a9afd-62a6-5b93-b7a2-483a714de544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505254} :answer-field/id #uuid "4354c19a-ced8-5d91-8370-a595dbdb9d13" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505642}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5baf8c7a-819a-521e-a261-67b2db30681d" :question/math-academy-id "q-341486" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}).$ **Do not round** your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}{1-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5}}{\\frac{5+\\sqrt{3}}{5}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,ln\\,(\\frac{5-\\sqrt{3}}{5+\\sqrt{3}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d68a11bd-7a2d-5f1f-8be7-c8904fdfb3e7" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d786de3f-c166-5197-8b45-69ebfef00b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505521} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7beb05c-ce8a-51e7-be2b-4e84b5896568" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5800963-d7c6-5c24-9f66-ca5ef86b26b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-341494" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $0<x<\\frac{1}{4},$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(4\\sqrt{x}-1) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+4\\sqrt{x}-1}{1-4\\sqrt{x}+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4\\sqrt{x}}{2-4\\sqrt{x}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2\\sqrt{x}}{1-2\\sqrt{x}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505812 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092291 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d68a11bd-7a2d-5f1f-8be7-c8904fdfb3e7" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Tangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+w}{1-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505285}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5999c02b-4e0a-5088-a1ce-199924772d19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2+w}{1-w}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505285} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c578e06-f08e-5dc7-b0a7-5b75a8b2c043" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c284afac-baa7-5076-92a3-0d89b2214f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-341500" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $-2<w<1,$ express $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic tangent.\n\n$\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{artanh}⁡x=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+x}{1-x}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{artanh}⁡(\\frac{2w+1}{3}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1+\\frac{2w+1}{3}}{1-\\frac{2w+1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\frac{3+2w+1}{3}}{\\frac{3-2w-1}{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4+2w}{2-2w}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2+w}{1-w}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50501d8e-9c4c-5650-a50f-5d847d29435a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Inverse Hyperbolic Function From First-Principles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b61954d0-ee42-5fdc-ac63-ec94caa6896b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b61954d0-ee42-5fdc-ac63-ec94caa6896b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505458} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea4e9517-341b-550d-a4d1-9501b5d7afb4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505718}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21de70c5-f015-584f-8044-af40301c4b75" :question/math-academy-id "q-341520" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(\\sqrt{x})$ for $x\\ge 0.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=($\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{x}.$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain. We have $y\\in [0,\\infty )$ for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡\\sqrt{y},\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $y\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of $\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-{e}^{-\\sqrt{y}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & ={e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the right-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2\\sqrt{y}}-2x{e}^{\\sqrt{y}}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})}^{2}-2x({e}^{\\sqrt{y}})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{\\sqrt{y}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{\\sqrt{y}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ \\sqrt{y} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & ={ln}^{2}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we require real $y$ and $x<\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}$ for all $x,$ we select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}))}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505763 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50501d8e-9c4c-5650-a50f-5d847d29435a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Inverse Hyperbolic Function From First-Principles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d0b88de0-5c37-5be3-b463-cbb0f5e3d938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\sqrt{\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505507}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0b88de0-5c37-5be3-b463-cbb0f5e3d938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\sqrt{\\ln\\left(x+\\sqrt{x^2-1}\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505507} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e116284-e68b-5144-aec0-ae224199df97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f32f1c5-eb40-50cf-b4aa-8b349651b9ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-341584" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic cosine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((x-2)^{2})$ and $x\\ge 2.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((x-2)^{2}).$ Since $x\\ge 2,$ we have $y\\ge 1.$\n\nTo find the inverse function, we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡((y-2)^{2}),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\ge 1$ and $y\\ge 2.$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,({e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+{e}^{-(y-2)^{2}}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+{e}^{-(y-2)^{2}} \\\\ 2x{e}^{(y-2)^{2}} & ={e}^{2(y-2)^{2}}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(y-2)^{2}}-2x{e}^{(y-2)^{2}}+1 & =0 \\\\ ({e}^{(y-2)^{2}})^{2}-2x({e}^{(y-2)^{2}})+1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{(y-2)^{2}}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}-1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ {e}^{(y-2)^{2}} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that for  $x\\ge 1,$ we have $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\le 1$ and  $x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}\\ge 1.$ Since the logarithm in the above equality must be positive, we reject the alternative with the negative sign. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\ y-2 & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})} \\\\ y & =2\\pm \\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want $y\\ge 2,$ select the positive solution. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1})}+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d3383cb-2322-5ed0-bd5b-631596d72af9" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "974fd4ef-27c2-5179-8fcf-ea2529f27fa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{m}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505397} :answer-field/id #uuid "e21bd3d7-6532-527f-ab53-721fcea93fbd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1203a643-2d1d-5bdd-a410-353b4bd6d719" :question/math-academy-id "q-342289" :question/problem "$$\n\nEvaluate $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}),$ for $m>0.$\n\n$\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m})}^{2}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16-8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}+1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{\\frac{16+8{m}^{2}+{m}^{4}}{16{m}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+|\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $m>0,$ we have $\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arsinh}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4-{m}^{2}}{4m}+\\frac{{m}^{2}+4}{4m}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{2}{m}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092293 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "50501d8e-9c4c-5650-a50f-5d847d29435a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Inverse Hyperbolic Function From First-Principles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e588604e-6614-597a-8dbe-b30656178764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e588604e-6614-597a-8dbe-b30656178764" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505554} :answer-field/id #uuid "19ea8a49-836d-5084-95fa-e50271405e3e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505622} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87af89b7-58f8-5c44-b8ea-5ae3a4eb74de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87af89b7-58f8-5c44-b8ea-5ae3a4eb74de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+\\sqrt{x^2+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505370} :answer-field/id #uuid "876e4b83-6790-5fe9-9bab-994d9c16699a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505669}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dec2af6f-6d06-5905-b90e-1ab8bd17313c" :question/math-academy-id "q-343418" :question/problem "Using the definition of the hyperbolic sine, calculate ${f}^{-1}(x)$ given that $f(x)=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-3).$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)+$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $y=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2x-3).$ Note that $f(x)$ is a one-to-one function on its entire domain, and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ for $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the inverse function we swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2y-3),\n$$\n\nwhere $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$ and $y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\nNext, we apply the definition of hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{2y-3}-{e}^{-(2y-3)}) \\\\ 2x & ={e}^{2y-3}-{e}^{-(2y-3)} \\\\ 2x{e}^{2y-3} & ={e}^{2(2y-3)}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBringing everything to the left-hand side, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(2y-3)}-2x{e}^{2y-3}-1 & =0 \\\\ {({e}^{2y-3})}^{2}-2x({e}^{2y-3})-1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $Y={e}^{2y-3}.$ We can form a quadratic equation in $Y$ and solve it by completing the square, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Y}^{2}-2xY-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2}-{x}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ (Y-x)^{2} & ={x}^{2}+1 \\\\ Y-x & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ Y & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {e}^{2y-3} & =x\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we require real $y,$ we must have ${e}^{2y-3}>0.$ However, $x-\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}<0$ for all real $x.$ Therefore, we select the positive solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back, we get\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1})+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092290 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "69ebb184-52aa-5fde-8956-50b69fde3561" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505553}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e56c770a-5806-5999-8f0f-05f21b960997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{p}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505553} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f2227fe-2623-5005-877d-8e648618036c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b0ffba6-735a-5d4b-89a4-021c8a5b9247" :question/math-academy-id "q-343590" :question/problem "$$\n\nGiven that $p\\ge 25,$ express $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})$ using the definition of the inverse hyperbolic cosine.\n\n$\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n{{field-1}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "The definition of $\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x$ is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p})}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}+50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}-1}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{\\frac{{p}^{4}-50{p}^{2}+625}{100{p}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p})}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+|\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}|)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $p\\ge 25,$ we have $\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}>0,$ and we can remove the absolute value in the above expression. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{arcosh}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{{p}^{2}+25}{10p}+\\frac{{p}^{2}-25}{10p}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{p}{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092289 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d3383cb-2322-5ed0-bd5b-631596d72af9" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Definition of Inverse Hyperbolic Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02f914b7-4ef9-5bba